welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn wilshire host and our guest is  Emily Erickson an associate professor of  sociology at Yale University she  conducts research in the fields of  social networks comparative historical  sociology organizations theory and  economics sociology her focus is on the  role of social networks and historical  and cultural change today we'll talk  with Professor Erickson about her new  book between monopoly and free trade the  English East India Company 1602 1757  which was the recipient of the Macmillan  centers 2016 gatos Smith international  book prize for best first book welcome  professor Ericson thanks so much for  having me let's begin with an overview  of your book tell us about it well the  book is about the English East India  Company the English East India Company  was a crown charter monopoly so it was  granted monopoly privileges to exclusive  trade in fact by Queen Elizabeth in 1601  to all the trade east of the Cape of  Good Hope and west of Cape Horn it went  over to the eastern search of spices  coffee tea cotton and it was a  commercial operation until about 1833 or  a monopoly commercial opposite operation  at which time it transferred into a the  foundation of the colonial empire or the  British colonial empire in Asia hmm but  I don't focus on the incipient  colonialism as much as I do on the  commercial aspects and what I found  really compelling about the East India  Company was that it allowed its  employees to engage in a private trade  so the employees traded they had their  own trading operations that they engaged  in in their own self-interest that was  outside of the company's interests so  it's as if you were working at a  restaurant and you and you were the  waitress but you sold drinks to the  customers  and and you profited from that rather  than than the restaurant itself so it's  an unusual unusual situation is it is  and what what sparked your interest in  this what led you to write the book oh  the the first thing that well the  private trade of course was compelling  but I was really interested in this sort  of age of encounters between Asia and  Europe I came out of a history degree at  UC Berkeley where I was introduced to  this incredible book called islands of  history by Marshall Sahlins and that was  about Captain Cook's voyages to Fiji  Hawaii and New Zealand and it's also  sort of about the way that encounters  between different societies can  transform the social structures of those  societies okay and I I just became very  interested in thinking about how these  events could have impacted the societies  civilizations in these in the West okay  so let's talk about about the employee  networks and why do you think the  company allowed their employees to do  such a thing and also were they selling  the company's goods how did that work or  it was their own goods that they were  selling talk a little bit about that oh  sure so essentially the company so this  was difficult it was difficult  conditions right so the employ I mean  they're traveling it takes six months at  least to get to Asia they you know you  have to live on a boat you're not with  your family scurvy was not under control  we're buying at this incredible rate so  it's a thirty-three percent mortality  rate for my business if you can imagine  a company like that nowadays wouldn't  get very far but so they had to offer  inducements to the employees and the  company the English company didn't it  was not in fact all that wealthy or  powerful when it began so the inducement  that they could offer was that they  would allow the employees to engage in  this private trainer and the private  trade was entirely funded by the capital  of the employees the captain's officers  and the semen to a very small extent  because of course you had to have  capital but so they supplied their own  capital their own goods and what were  the goods what were often it was liquor  so Iraq a kind of a liquor there was  mechanical trinkets were very popular  and Wow often also jewels wright and  jewels were attractive to people because  there's a lot of wealth in a compact  form right so that those were the  central commodities okay interesting so  how did you do the the research for the  book I mean how did you go about finding  out about this kind of stuff oh well so  the research so what I did that what was  different that I did in this book was i  use network analysis to illuminate the  in-form patterns of communication  between employees so in order to do  network analysis you have to get  systematic data if that's what was  difficult about that or about the the  research process was I had to find some  kind of systematic data about encounters  between these European and Asian markets  so what I did essentially was I just  combed the stacks I was at Colombian  graduate school so I combed the sacks at  butler library until I found these  incredible volumes and it was sort of  like a gold mine they were a record of  all of a record of the logs of the  journeys of the voyages of the English  East India Company East Indiamen chips  okay so once I got that and then there  was a record of all of the officers  careers also so once I got that then I  had to convert it into electronic format  okay this is something that is so  commonplace now but ten years ago it was  actually a pretty cumbersome process  what was the problem what was the  process what did you have to do I had to  scan all of the pages convert it this  was this was actually right when  converting PDF to text has just been  sort of invented right it would have  just it was really new technology and so  I converted it to text and then I  actually I learned to program so that I  could reorganize all the data although  it still took so many so many I mean  months of cleaning sadly but it's much  faster now you know it's much easier to  engage in this kind of research now so  how did the English East India Company  become one  of the most prominent and enduring  organizations in history well it's a  great question it's an important  question because it sort of flies in the  face of what we think is important about  organizations in general so in sociology  often is sort of ave burien max favor  legacy to think of organizations was all  about hierarchical control but in this  case and this is really one of the most  important earliest companies in history  it wasn't about hierarchical control it  was essentially about ceding power and  autonomy to the employees so they  essentially profit shared by seeding the  exclusive privileges that had been  granted to the company to the employees  they gave the employees all this  autonomy to engage in this private trade  and that translated into informal  collaboration between the employees so  it's really it's profit-sharing autonomy  and collaboration and I would imagine  that also helped the bottom line for the  the English east india company itself  because they had a many employees out in  the world you know doing business  basically absolutely absolutely so those  patterns of behavior they produced the  success of the English East India  Company they made it competitive  compared to the other firms because  drawing on the innovative capacity of  the employees and the way that they  distributed information throughout the  company the firm itself became more  flexible and it was able to adapt to  what we're really volatile market  conditions in overseas trade at that  time did you look at any of the people  who who were at the top who were heading  the company and any history about them  oh well the people that were heading the  company were so interested they were all  against the private trade of course  because they wanted to get as much as  they could out of their exclusive  privileges but they in state they they  couldn't help in order to keep the  company moving they couldn't help but  grant privileges to the employees but  they were all against a so it was  interesting it was an unintended  consequence okay  and so another outcome of this is it  really expanded the trade between Europe  and Asia can you talk a little bit about  what you found there as well oh sure  that's another really great question  because it reveals something I mean the  reason that the English east india  company became a dominant force and  trade between Asia and Europe is really  because of the sophisticated markets  that existed in Asia before before the  English he's sending a company went  there or before any of these large  chartered European companies so  especially the private trade really  depended upon pre-existing merchant  networks and the availability of small  capital loans so essentially what they  needed were open markets would with  pretty sophisticated financial  techniques and and those were were to be  found in spades across Asia which was a  very developed rich sophisticated  commercial world so it was really the  sophistication and complexity of the  markets in Asia that supported the  success of the English east india  company over time and allowed it to  become such such an important figure in  this overseas trade okay and what do you  conclude in your book so I think there's  there's a number of conclusions but the  the ultimate conclusion that i want to  emphasize is that it was the informal  patterns of collaboration between the  company that that between the company  employees that produce the success of  the company so what you can pull out  from that that I think fascinating is  that social networks social networks  that we think of as sort of  interpersonal communication can actually  have these huge impacts on the course of  human history so in this case they  influence through the success of the  company the trajectory of global trade  and politics for centuries and you can  argue that it affected the trajectory of  global trade and politics until brexit  and the end of you know British hedge  ammonia so so these little things  cumulate up and two big outcomes right  interesting so your book is out now yes  and I know you're working on a new  project tell us about it oh so my new  project I'm very excited about it it's  about the development  economic thought in the 17th century and  I have a lot of evidence that shows that  it's actually very closely tied to the  rise of Chartered companies at the same  time of which the English East India  Company was one of these charter  companies so are you looking all over  the world globally or are you focused on  basically that the colonial empire oh  yeah I am focused it's mostly so  economic thought is mostly an English  phenomenon at this time so I do a little  bit of a comparative cases especially  with the Dutch Republic because you  think that the Dutch were a little more  advanced than the English at that time  you would think they would have produced  economic thought but they didn't make  any significant contributions  interesting and they also didn't really  have the same number of companies and  they not it was not as widespread in the  Netherlands which i think is really one  of the keys to to this sort of puzzle  right right well we will look forward to  that book coming out in the near future  thanks so much and it's been great to  have you it's been great to be here  thank you for more information about  professor Erickson and her research  please visit our website at macmillan  report yale edu be sure to join us again  for another episode of the macmillan  report made possible through funding  from the Whitney and Betty Macmillan  Center for International and area  studies at Yale  you
 

